  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Milwaukee eviscerated the Padres in the opening inning and the game was over by the second.",
    "what_this_means": "Griffin Canning exited after 1.2 innings with six runs charged to his ledger. The bullpen stabilized immediately. Marinaccio, Matsui, and Waldron combined for 6.1 scoreless frames with seven strikeouts. But the deficit was already insurmountable. The offense managed seven hits and scored once on 11 runners in scoring position. Nick Castellanos went 2-for-4, but that stat line counted for nothing in a game decided before the lineup ever threatened to catch up. The Brewers' early assault left the Padres chasing from the first pitch, and chasers rarely close gaps this wide.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what happens when the starter collapses before the middle innings even arrive. The bullpen was sharp enough to win most games; instead it mopped up a blowout. Canning's implosion didn't expose the offense or the back end. It just made them irrelevant. The pitching gave away the game in roughly 40 minutes. Nine strikeouts and ten hits later, the Padres had nothing to show for it. The division saw them slip, and they have no excuse to offer.",
